// Model S and Model X from before the 2021 refresh ("legacy": 2012-2020, the
// first Model X from late 2015), read after opendbc's tesla_can.dbc (chassis bus)
// and tesla_powertrain.dbc (powertrain bus), which have only what openpilot needs.
//
// The messages below are written from Open Vehicle Monitoring System 3's Model S
// module (MIT, LICENSE-ovms.txt; vehicle/OVMS.V3/components/vehicle_teslamodels/
// src/vehicle_teslamodels.cpp, repository HEAD 2755ca51f2833af5093a4260ed74e11b2fd936db),
// each signal's bits, scale and unit as that code reads them; OVMS reads 0x102 to
// 0x6F2 on CAN3 (powertrain) and 0x31F on CAN6 (chassis). OVMS reads the VIN from
// 0x508 (first byte 0, 1, 2: seven, seven and three characters): Sentry Six Manager
// reads it there to tell the car (server/carModels.js), so it has no signals here.
//
// NOT CHECKED ON A REAL CAR: nobody has sent a legacy Model S or X log yet. These
// come from the community sources above only.
//
// Two ids of the powertrain and chassis files clash with one here or with each
// other (one id, one message here): 0x238 is the powertrain's STW_ACTN_RQ (the
// chassis's UI_driverAssistRoadSign is left out), and 0x338 is OVMS's range
// message on the powertrain bus (the chassis's UI_status bits are left out).
//
// 0x116 and 0x118 (DI_torque2, powertrain and chassis): as opendbc has them, but
// the speed in km/h (opendbc: mph, 0.05 mph steps from -25 mph), as every other
// model's DI_vehicleSpeed, so the stats and the computed ones read it alike.

